<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canute VI of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1163">1163</a>–1202</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canute VI was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from 1182 to 1202, the son of Valdemar I. With his adviser Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> he extended Danish power along the southern Baltic, forcing the Pomeranian duke Bogislaw I to become his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in 1185. He refused homage to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and was succeeded by his brother Valdemar II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canute-vi-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Canute VI of Denmark" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hideko Bondesen · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nordenskirker_Stehag(57).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Canute VI was the eldest son of King Valdemar I and Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>. On 25 June 1170, at the age of seven, he was proclaimed and crowned co-king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> with his father, in the first coronation in Danish history, performed by Archbishop Eskil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> at Ringsted. In 1177 he married Gertrude, daughter of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; the marriage produced no children. Contemporary sources describe him as an earnest, strongly religious man.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father's death in 1182 Canute became sole ruler. He at once faced a peasant uprising in Skåne, where the peasants refused to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a>'s tithe. The nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a> and Skåne defeated them at Dösjebro, and Canute then punished the region with fire and sword so relentlessly that Absalon begged him to stop.</p><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> had compelled Canute's father to acknowledge him as overlord. In 1184 he demanded the same of Canute. Canute did not reply. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> answered for him that Canute was as free a king as the emperor. The emperor ordered his vassal Bogislaw I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> to invade, and Bogislaw gathered 500 ships. Absalon assembled the Danish fleet and, according to the account, attacked the Pomeranians in fog, capturing 35 ships. Canute ordered two invasions of Pomerania and in 1185 forced Bogislaw to acknowledge him as overlord. From then until 1972 the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> used the title King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>. In 1197 Canute personally led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His brother Valdemar, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Southern Jutland</a>, was twelve when their father died, and Bishop Valdemar of Schleswig was appointed his regent. The bishop plotted with Count Adolf III of Holstein against the king. In 1192 the young duke had the bishop arrested, and he was held in Søborg Tower for thirteen years. Valdemar defeated Adolf at the Battle of Stellau in 1201 and captured him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a>, Canute's friend and chief adviser, died on 21 March 1201, and Canute died suddenly on 12 November 1202 at the age of 39. As he had no children, he was succeeded by his brother Valdemar II, who ruled from 1202 to 1241.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canute VI of Denmark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
